  [38;5;153mactor, at, before, after}[39m. Task state is a snapshot kept alongside. The bell computes your notifications by scanning
  events since your [38;5;153mlastReadAt[39m watermark, filtered by your subscriptions. [3mWins when[0m notification rules are still in
  flux: change what counts as notification-worthy and the whole history re-derives correctly, including retroactively.
  Costs derivation logic and eventual log pruning. Ports to a server essentially unchanged.

  [1mB. Materialized notification records.[0m On mutation, a hook writes one notification row per subscribed user. The bell
  just reads rows. [3mWins on[0m read simplicity — this is what most production trackers actually do at scale. Costs
  duplicated state, and rule changes only affect future events; you can't retroactively fix a bad filter.

  [1mC. Diff-on-write watermark.[0m No log — just [38;5;153mupdatedAt[39m per task and a per-user [38;5;153mlastSeen[39m. [3mI'm discarding this one[0m: it
  can't distinguish a status change from a title edit, and you explicitly asked for status-specific triggers. It fails
  the requirement outright.

  [1mMy recommendation is A[0m, with B available later as a pure cache. The reason is specific to where you are: you picked
  "status + due date" as triggers on instinct, and flagged the others as tunable. A is the option where tuning that
  filter later is a config change instead of a migration. Local data volume is trivially small — a few hundred events —
  so deriving on read costs nothing measurable, and if it ever does, materializing rows becomes a cache in front of the
  same source of truth rather than a rewrite.

  It also directly serves the "while you were away" catch-up you chose: that view [3mis[0m a query over the log, so it falls
  out for free rather than being a separate feature.
